Both Random Forest and XGBoost produced encouraging results. Random Forest achieved a test ROC-AUC of 83.48%, while XGBoost achieved 84.17%. The XGBoost model therefore exceeded the required 80% benchmark and was selected as the preferred model based on its stronger ROC-AUC and recall performance.

The feature importance analysis also provided useful insight into the variables influencing model predictions, particularly contract type, tenure, internet services and customer billing characteristics.

From a business perspective, the project demonstrated how predictive analytics can help telecommunications organisations identify customers who may be at risk of leaving and support more informed customer retention strategies.

